program: A set of instructions a computer excutes to a perform a task.

Source Code: Human-readable code written by a programmer. 

Syntax: The rules governing how code must be written.

Semantics: The meaning/behavior of valid code.

Statement: A complete instruction executed by a program. 
    x = x + 1;
Expression: Code that produces a value.
    x + 5
Literal: A value written directly into source code. 

Identifier: A name used to identify a program entity.
    myVar
Keyword: A reserved word with a special meaning in a language.
    class, if, return, template, int, etc
Comment: Text ignored by the compiler, used to document code. 
    //line in c++, /*multi-line in c++*/, #line in python, """ multi-line in python """
Bug: An unknown defect in a program

Debugging: Finding and fixing bugs.

Runtime: The period during which a program is executing.

Compile time: The period when source code is translated/checked before execution.


Variables and data types
Variable: A named object/storage location whose value can change. 
    int age = 24;
Constant: A value that cannot be modified after initialization. 
    const int MAX = 100;
Data: Information manipulated by a program. 
    number, text, images, etc
Data type: Defines what kind of data something represents and how it can be used. 
    int, double, bool, char, unsigned_int, struct, class
Primitive type: A fundamental built-in data type
    int, char, bool
Integer: A whole number

Floating-point: A type representing numbers with fractional values.
    double pi = 3.14159
Boolean: A value representing true or false.

Character: A single character.

String: A sequence of characters.

Null/nullptr: Represents the absence of an object/reference for a pointer-like value.
    int* p = nullptr;

Type Conversion: Changing a value from one type to another. 

Type Inference: Allowing the compiler to determine a variable's type. 
    auto in c++


Operators
Operator: A symbol or keyword that performs an operation.
    !, +, =, ==, etc
Arithmetic operator: preforms mathematical operations. 
    a * b
Assignment: Gives a variable a value.
    x = 10;
Comparison: Compares value.
    x == 10;
Logical Operator: Combines or modifies Boolean cconditions.
    x > 0 && y > 0
Unary operator: operates on one operand. 
    !x, ++x, -x;
Binary operator: Operates on two operands
    x + y;
Ternary operator: Conditional operator involving three operands. 
    x > 0 ? 1 : -1

Control Flow
Control flow: The oder in which instructions execute.
    if, else, loop, return
Conditional: Code tht executes depending on a condition. 
    if(x > 10)
Condition: An expression evaluated as true or false. 
    x == 5
if statement: Executes code when a condition is true.
    if (age >= 18) {...}
else: provides an alternative branch

else if: Tests another condition if the previous one was false.

Switch: Selects among the multiple cases based on a value. 

Loop: Repeatedly executes code.

Iteration: One repettion of a loop:

for loop: Loop commonly used when iteration structure is known, 

While loop: Repeats while a condition is true.

Break: Immediately exits a loop or switch.

Continue: Skips to the next loop iteration. 

Recursion: A function calls itself. 

Functions
Function: A function packages a resuable operation.

Functon declaration: Tells the compiler a function exists.
    int add( int s, int p) 
Function definition: Provides the implementation of a function
    int add(int a, int b){return a+b}
Parameter: Variable specified in a function definition. 
    int a
Argument: Actual value passed to a function.
    add(2,3)->2
Return value: Value produced by a function.
    return a + b;
Return type: Type  of a value a function returns. 
  return *int*;
Void: Indicates that a function returns no value.

Function call: Invoking a function.
    add(2,3);
Overloading: Multiple functions share a name but have a different parameters.

Callback: A function supplied to another function to be invoked later.

higher-order function: A function that accepts or return another functon.

Scope and lifetime (c++)
Scope: Region of code where a name can be accessed.
    A local variable exists within its {} block.
local variable: Variable declared inside a function/block

Global Variable: variable declared outside functions/classes.

Lifetime: how long an object exsts durng program execution.
    pointers
Namespace: A named scope used to organize and distinguish identifiers. 

Visibility: Whether a particular piece of code can access an entity. 
    private, public, etc

Memory
Memory: Storage used by a computer to hold program data
        RAM
Address: Location of data in memory.

Pointer: Object containing a memory address.

Reference: An alias for another object.

Dereference: Accessing the object pointed to by a pointer.

Address-of operator: Obtains an object's address.

Heap: Memory region generally used for dynamcally allocated objects. 

Stack: Memory traditionally used for function calls and automatic objects.

Dynamic allocation: Obtaining memory during program execution. 

Deallocation: Releasing dynamically allocated memory. 

Memory leak: Allocated memory that is no longer reachable/reclaimable. 
        haning pointers
Dangling pointer: Pointer referring to an object that no longer exists.

RAII: c++ technique where resource lifetime is tied to object lifetime.
        std::unique_ptr automatically releases memory.


Data Structures
Data Structures: Organization of data for efficient use. 

Array: Contiguous sequence of elements, usually fixed-size. 

Vector: Dynamically sized array.

List: Collection whose elements are linked through nodes.

Stack: Last in first out (LIFO) structure.

Queue: FIFO Structure: First in, first out. 

Deque: Double-ended queue. 

Set: Collection of unique values. 

Map: collection Associating keys with values. 

Hash table: Structure providing fast average-case key lookup. 

Tree: Hierarchical data structure.
        file-system
Graph: Collection of vertices connected by edges.

Node: Individual element within many linked structures. 

Object-Oriented Programming
Object:  An instanace containing data and/or behavior

Class: Type (blueprint) defining an object's structure and behavior. 

Member: Element belonging to a class/object.

Member variable: Data stored within an object.

Member function: Function belonging to a class.

Constructor: Special function used to initialize an object. 

Destructor: Special function called when an object is destroyed.

Encapsulation: Bundling data and behavior while controlling access. 

Inheritance: Creating a type based on another type.

Polymorphism: Allowing one interface to represent  different underlyng implementations.

Abstraction: Hiding implementation details behind a simpler interface.

Virtual function: C++ function supportng runtime polymorphism. 

Compostion: Building an object from other objects. 


C++ Specific Vocabulary
Template: Code parameterized types or values.

Type Parameter: A templatenparameter representing a type. 

typename: Indicates that a dependent name represents a type; also introduces tyoe temokate parameters. 

class: Can also introduce a type template parameter.

Dependent type: A type whose meaning dependds on a template parameter. 

insantiation: Creating a concrete verssion of a template. 

Generic Programming: Writing algorithms/data structures that work with many types.

STL: Common C++ library componets for containers, algorithms, iterators, ...

Iterator: Object used to traverse elements in a range/container. 

lvalue: An expression referring to an identifiable object/location.
        int x
rvalue: Generally, a temorary/value expression that isn't a lvalue.
        x + 1
Move Semantics: Allows resources to be transferred rather than copied. 
        std::move(obj)
Smart Pointer: Pointer-like object managing ownership automatically.

Header: File Containing declarations, definitions, or interfaces.
        #include <vector>
Translation Unit: A source file after preprocessing, treated as one compilation unit. 
        main.cpp
Linker: Combines compiled code and resolves references between translation units/libraries.

ABI: Binary interface governing how complied componets interact. 


Compilation
Compilation: Converts source code into lower-level code.
